import { execSync } from 'node:child_process';
import { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const src = execSync('git -C /home/user/Claudinite show HEAD~1:packs/basics/RULES.md', { encoding: 'utf8' });
const orig = src.slice(src.indexOf('# Engineering practices')).split('\n')
  .filter((l) => l.startsWith('- ')).map((l) => l.slice(2));

// group sizes: how many final rules each original bullet became
const groups = [1, 8, 1, 3, 5, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 2, 2, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 5, 2, 4, 4, 1, 3];

const rules = [
  // 1
  'When you name a file, module, or symbol, make sure to name it by scope or responsibility, never by technology or mechanism.',
  // 2
  'When you add data that is derived from, or duplicates, data you already have, make sure to keep one source of truth, generate the rest from it, and add a test that fails on drift.',
  'When you are about to write a fact into a second place, make sure a technological constraint genuinely forbids a single home — otherwise put it in one.',
  'When you write the drift-guard test for a split you had to make, make sure its own text names the two-or-more places it watches and why the split is forced — otherwise the next reader can\'t tell the duplication is deliberate or find its other half.',
  'When you drift-guard a duplicate, and the thing it mirrors is executable logic you can\'t generate from (a static list shadowing predicate/matcher functions), make sure the guard *executes* the real logic against the list rather than parsing the logic out.',
  'When you write that executing guard, make sure it runs both directions — every entry accepted by some predicate, and every predicate satisfied by some entry.',
  'When you copy a value into a second file, and the two can\'t share an import, make sure to declare a `sharedConstants` entry in `.claudinite-checks.json` with its `value`, per-file `counts`, and a `what` naming the places and why.',
  'When you write or move an occurrence of a `sharedConstants`-guarded value, make sure it sits on one line — otherwise the *flat*-literal count (comments included) misses it, and so does a `grep`/`sed` rename.',
  'When you declare a `sharedConstants` entry, and the value changes over time (a version string bumped each release), make sure to set `regex: true` and give a pattern — otherwise the guard needs re-pinning on every bump.',
  // 3
  'When you write code comments or Markdown in file A, and A uses or references file B, make sure A states only *what* it needs from B or *that* it delegates — never *how* B does its job — otherwise change B and A\'s paraphrase silently goes stale.',
  // 4
  'When you stage a commit, and it holds two changes that could each stand alone (separately revertible, cherry-pickable, bisectable), make sure to split them into separate commits.',
  'When you write a commit message, and find yourself numbering unrelated items, make sure to split the commit instead; more commits is not itself the goal.',
  'When you revise work that has already landed, make sure the revision is a *new* commit, never a rewrite of the one that is in.',
  // 5
  'When you add a test that autogenerates a file, make sure `GENERATED` is in that file\'s name (`foo.GENERATED.md`, `foo.baseline.GENERATED.json`).',
  'When you are about to edit a file, and its name says `GENERATED`, make sure you change the generator instead — otherwise the file desyncs from its source.',
  'When you resolve a merge conflict, and the conflicted file is GENERATED, make sure to take either side to clear the markers, re-run the generator against the merged inputs, and commit its output — otherwise, if the other branch changed the generator, the values are wrong.',
  'When you find yourself clearing those markers by hand, make sure the file gets a `merge=ours` `.gitattributes` entry, plus a one-time `git config merge.ours.driver true` per clone.',
  'When you resolve a conflict by hand that will recur, make sure `git config rerere.enabled true` is set — otherwise you resolve the same conflict again next time.',
  // 6
  'When you write code that depends on how a platform or runtime behaves, make sure to verify that behaviour against authoritative docs or a real run, never against a comment or a prior commit\'s claim.',
  // 7
  'When you land an optimisation meant to preserve behaviour, make sure to hash the full outputs of the old and new paths across *every* branch (fuzzing the inputs that select each) and accept only a bit-identical match — otherwise "it should be equivalent" is all the evidence you have.',
  'When you decide not to optimise a hot path because rewriting it risks correctness more than it buys speed, make sure to record that as a deliberate call — otherwise it reads as an oversight.',
  // 8
  'When you add a dependency for a narrow job, make sure a built-in — or a few lines — won\'t do it first.',
  'When you change code resting on the assumption a dependency was added for, and that assumption no longer holds, make sure to drop the dependency.',
  // 9
  'When you start writing production code for an edge case a review raised ("what about scenario X?"), make sure to first check whether the existing composed rules already produce the right answer.',
  'When that check shows the rules already cover it, make sure to add no production code — pin it with a regression test, plus an accept criterion under the relevant requirement where you track those.',
  'When you write that regression test, make sure it asserts the contrast case too — otherwise it won\'t bite if the rule interaction changes.',
  // 10
  'When you write a bespoke how-to, make sure to read the authoritative external docs first and keep only what they don\'t carry — our failure modes, gotchas, the exact path we ran — otherwise it re-explains upstream, usually less well.',
  'When you write down a recommended path, and you have not actually run it, make sure you don\'t — however standard or better it looks.',
  // 11
  'When you add error handling during development, and the solution isn\'t proven right yet, make sure to add debugging information first and remove it later — otherwise the handler swallows the errors and you lose observability exactly where you need it.',
  // 12
  'When you apply a capability best-effort behind a silent fallback (a probe-gated optimisation, an optional platform feature, a hint the runtime may ignore), make sure to record whether it actually engaged — otherwise a run where it never engaged is indistinguishable from one where it did and didn\'t help.',
  'When you write that record, make sure it reads the state back where the platform exposes it and logs which path ran.',
  // 13
  'When you write code that persists something on a user\'s machine, make sure it goes under the **one** user-deletable location — otherwise "delete that folder and it\'s gone" stops being true, and users act on that promise.',
  'When you add a new kind of persisted state, make sure to extend that location rather than create a second one.',
  'When you persist something the platform forces outside that location (a registration the OS owns), make sure to name it explicitly as the exception.',
  // 14
  'When you write a permission usage string, a privacy policy, or a store-listing disclosure, make sure to treat it as part of the behaviour\'s contract, not documentation of it.',
  'When you change what the software does with a user\'s data, make sure the statement changes **in the same commit** — otherwise a stale disclosure ships, which is worse than a blunt one.',
  'When you write code that retains something previously not retained, adds an outbound connection, or opens a listener, make sure that is an explicit decision and the disclosure is rewritten before the code — it changes the *promise*, not just a field.',
  'When you change behaviour a standing absolute speaks to ("no tracking", "no cookies", "no external assets"), make sure to grep the whole surface and reconcile every hit in that same commit — otherwise nothing fails when one restatement becomes untrue.',
  // 15
  'When you write a script to drive an external runtime (a headless browser, a device, a REPL, a deploy target), and the session will drive it more than once, make sure to write **one** parameterised driver into the scratchpad and re-point it.',
  'When you write that driver, make sure it takes the target, the selector, and the output path from argv — otherwise you re-author a throwaway per invocation and re-derive the same setup each time.',
  // 16
  'When you automate a task, and the task needs live AI conversation context (a reflection pass over a session), make sure to hook it to an existing human workflow event, never to background infrastructure — shell hooks have no conversation access and fire per-turn, not at session-end.',
  'When you pick that hook, make sure to try changing an existing command\'s definition first — it is usually the cheapest.',
  // 17
  'When you write the exit path for a pipeline or CI step, and the outcome is expected and handled (a known-bad input, an anticipated stop), make sure it exits clean with a comment, not as a failure.',
  'When you write a step that exits non-zero, make sure the condition is genuine breakage — otherwise a red run stops being a surprise.',
  // 18
  'When you debug a fetch that works on your machine but 403/400s or hits a CAPTCHA wall from CI or a sandbox, make sure to suspect the *datacenter IP*, not the User-Agent — browser-like headers won\'t change it.',
  'When you fix that fetch, make sure to route it through a residential-proxy service rather than tweak headers — these usually render JS too, so a single-page app records real content.',
  'When you plan to cache a target, and it stays blocked even through a proxy, make sure to treat it as un-cacheable.',
  // 19
  'When you write a command containing `pkill -f "<pattern>"`, make sure nothing is chained after it — it matches the *invoking shell\'s own* command line too, so the shell dies and everything after the `;` or `&&` is silently dropped.',
  'When you write a `-f` pkill pattern, make sure to bracket one character (`pkill -f "[h]ttp.server 8099"`) to break the self-match.',
  // 20
  'When you write a cloud or root setup script, make sure it `cd`s into the checkout before running anything — otherwise it may start in the repo\'s *parent* directory, where a bare `npm ci` finds no manifest and silently does nothing.',
  // 21
  'When you debug a missing-dependency error (`Cannot find module …`), and the checkout is *fresh*, make sure to run the install and re-run before hunting for a code-level cause — vendored dependency dirs start empty on a clone.',
  // 22
  'When you add a way to track a property already tracked elsewhere (a spec tag, a side manifest, a per-item field), make sure to collapse them into a single source of truth.',
  'When you choose where that property lives, make sure to prefer a *structural* classifier derived from where the item lives over a hand-set field — otherwise there is a field to forget to update.',
  // 23
  'When you write a fallback for a value that wasn\'t supplied, make sure to require the value explicitly or make it structural instead — otherwise the real value lives in a fallback plus a comment, two things that drift and neither visible at the call site.',
  'When you write automation that maintains many instances of a config (a fleet of repos, generated project files), make sure it **materializes the explicit value into every file it maintains** rather than interpret absence — otherwise the knob isn\'t in the file anyone would go to change.',
  // 24
  'When you encode a value that may be unknown, make sure you never encode the unknown as a zero, an empty string, or a type\'s default — it is a state of its own.',
  'When you write a decoder for a field that can legitimately be absent, make sure it keeps unknown as a missing key or an explicit null and leaves the zero meaning a real zero.',
  'When you write a pipeline stage that passes such a value on, make sure it preserves all three states (absent, zero, a real value) — otherwise it folds three into two, loses what nothing downstream can recover, and fails silently.',
  'When you write the branch that handles the absent case, make sure it treats absence as a normal, permanent shape of the data, not a gap to close.',
  'When you write a stage that sees two fields making overlapping claims about one fact (a `free` flag beside a `price: 0`), make sure each keeps meaning what its own source said and the consumer decides.',
  // 25
  'When you write a check that walks a file tree (an orphan/reachability scan, a doc audit, a lint), and it must ignore files you don\'t own, make sure its file set comes from `git ls-files`, never a filesystem walk with a hardcoded path-to-skip.',
